# Code Conventions

## Naming

- Use meaningful names for variables, functions, and classes.
- Abbreviations or single-character variable names are prohibited, except for loop indices (`i`, `j`).

## Indentation

- CSS/HTML/JS/TS/TSX/JSX: 2 spaces

## Line Length

- Limit each line to a maximum of 120 characters.

## Comments

- Write comments only for complex logic, business rules, or non-obvious intent.
- Do not write comments that simply describe what the code does.

```ts
// ❌ BAD
// Returns the user's name
function getUserName() { ... }

// ✅ GOOD
// Re-registration with the same email is blocked for 30 days after account deletion
function isEmailBlocked(email: string) { ... }
```

## Function / Class Independence

- Each function and class should have a single responsibility and be written to be as independent as possible.
- Side effects must be clearly separated.

## No Repeated Code / No Hardcoding

- Extract repeated logic into functions or hooks.
- Fixed values must always be separated out as constants.

```ts
// ❌ BAD
if (role === 'admin') { ... }

// ✅ GOOD
const ROLES = { ADMIN: 'admin', USER: 'user' } as const;
if (role === ROLES.ADMIN) { ... }
```

## Minimize Code Depth

- Avoid nested `if-else` statements and nested callbacks.
- Use the early return pattern.

```ts
// ❌ BAD
function process(user) {
  if (user) {
    if (user.isActive) {
      return user.name;
    }
  }
}

// ✅ GOOD
function process(user) {
  if (!user) return null;
  if (!user.isActive) return null;
  return user.name;
}
```

## Error Handling

- Errors must be conveyed to the user with meaningful messages.
- Empty `catch` blocks are strictly forbidden.

```ts
// ❌ BAD
try {
  await fetchData();
} catch (e) {}

// ✅ GOOD
try {
  await fetchData();
} catch (e) {
  console.error('Failed to load data.', e);
  throw new Error('Data load failed');
}
```

## Security / Infrastructure

- Secret keys, API tokens, and environment configuration must always be managed via environment variables (`.env`).
- Always validate and escape user input.
- Specify dependency versions explicitly, and periodically check for vulnerability updates.
